  block txt_sum_why: TextBlock {
    content: @md **What the difference does.** Every column shifts at once here, and Cohort Size shifts with them — which is the clearest sign that the second filter is selecting a different population rather than trimming a report.

**The second report describes a different population.** Filtering at the final-results level selects everyone who *ever* bought the category rather than those who bought it at acquisition, so Cohort Size itself moves: for Jan 2026 / Skin Care it reads 210 against 83. Month 0 is where it shows plainest — every acquired customer orders in their acquisition month by definition, so retention there is 100% when the filter profiles the cohort, and 44.8% when it only trims the output.;;
  }

  block txt_ov_hero: TextBlock {
    content: @md <div style="border-left:4px solid #05264C;padding:4px 0 4px 18px">
<h1 style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:26px;color:#05264C">What this dashboard is showing</h1>
<p style="margin:0;font-size:15px;color:#505157;line-height:1.6">
A <strong>cohort</strong> is simply a group of customers who all started at the same time. Everyone who made their first purchase in January is the January cohort. We then follow that group month by month.
</p>
</div>

That lets us answer the question every business eventually asks:

> **"When we spend money to win a customer, do they stick around — and what are they actually worth to us?"**

Each tab answers one version of it: do they come back (**Retention**), how much do they spend (**LTV**), how often do they order (**Orders Per User**), and so on.;;
  }

  block txt_ov_problem: TextBlock {
    content: @md ## Then someone asks a sharper question

*"Fine — but what about the customers we won with skincare? Were they any good?"*

This is where dashboards quietly go wrong. There are **two ways to apply that filter**. They look identical on screen. Only one answers the question that was asked.;;
  }

  block txt_ov_right_card: TextBlock {
    content: @md <div style="background:#F5F8FA;border-left:4px solid #1B7CE4;border-radius:6px;padding:16px 18px;height:100%">
<div style="font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1B7CE4;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:8px">Cohort filter</div>
<div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#05264C;margin-bottom:12px">Use it to pick the group</div>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px;font-size:14px;color:#212327;line-height:1.6">
“Customers whose <strong>first purchase</strong> included skincare.”
</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px;font-size:14px;color:#505157;line-height:1.6">
Choose the group <strong>first</strong> — then measure everything they ever bought afterwards. The shampoo, the sofa, the phone case: all of it still counts, because all of it came from a customer skincare won for us.
</p>
<div style="font-size:13px;color:#1F815A;font-weight:600">Answers: was skincare a good way to win customers?</div>
</div>;;
  }

  block txt_ov_wrong_card: TextBlock {
    content: @md <div style="background:#FFF7EA;border-left:4px solid #E36A01;border-radius:6px;padding:16px 18px;height:100%">
<div style="font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#B04A00;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:8px">Result filter</div>
<div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#05264C;margin-bottom:12px">Use it to trim the answer</div>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px;font-size:14px;color:#212327;line-height:1.6">
“Show me <strong>skincare sales</strong>.”
</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px;font-size:14px;color:#505157;line-height:1.6">
Measure everybody first — then throw away every line on the report that isn’t skincare. The group was never narrowed, and everything else those customers bought has just been deleted from view.
</p>
<div style="font-size:13px;color:#B04A00;font-weight:600">Answers: how much skincare did we sell?</div>
</div>;;
  }

  block txt_ov_numbers: TextBlock {
    content: @md ## The same question, two very different answers

Here is the January 2026 cohort, filtered to skincare both ways. Same data, same metric, same screen.

| | Cohort filter | Result filter |
|---|---|---|
| **Customers in the group** | 83 | 210 |
| **Buying in month 0** | 100.0% | 44.8% |
| **Still buying in month 1** | 30.1% | 12.4% |
| **Worth per customer by month 6** | $833.03 | $207.05 |

Read the right-hand column on its own and skincare looks like a failure — barely one in eight customers comes back, and each is worth a quarter as much. You would stop the campaign.

It is not true. Those customers **did** come back. They just bought something other than skincare, and the report quietly threw that away.

<div style="background:#F5F8FA;border-left:4px solid #1B7CE4;border-radius:6px;padding:14px 18px;margin-top:8px">
<strong style="color:#05264C">The giveaway is the very first row.</strong> Month 0 is the month these customers joined — <em>everyone</em> buys in the month they join, by definition. It has to be 100%. When it reads 44.8%, the group on screen is not the group you think you are looking at.
</div>;;
  }

  block txt_ov_try: TextBlock {
    content: @md ## See it for yourself

1. Open any tab — **Retention** is the clearest
2. Set **Category** to `Skin Care`
3. That one filter feeds **both** reports at once. Watch them separate.

There is no trick in the filter. You picked one category, once. The top report uses it to **choose the group**; the bottom uses it to **trim the finished numbers** — and that single difference is worth four times the answer.

**Both filters are multi-select, and they combine.** Pick `Skin Care` *and* `Clothes` to profile customers won by either. Add a **Product** or two on top, then a **Country**, and you have narrowed to a specific group of customers — every filter you add tightens the same group, and the reports keep answering for their whole history.

*Cohort Month, Lifecycle Window, Country and Units Band narrow the group correctly whichever report they touch. Only Category and Product behave differently between the two.*;;
  }

